diff --git a/collab/QUESTIONS.md b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
index 2435ff6..d08cb4e 100644
--- a/collab/QUESTIONS.md
+++ b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
@@ -1177,3 +1177,5 @@ One line per product decision made while building, newest last.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-14 (links): the floor is that a link may not cost what ⌃⌥Z cannot bring back. Above it, `arrange`, its styled forms on a whole display, `cycle-layout` and `layout` stay refused where they would cost a saved view or a name, although undo restores them. What takes several links and has a way back each time is not guarded. `arbor://save-view/N` is refused where N is taken.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-14 (`arborctl … > /dev/full`): by design: the exit code says whether Arbor did it, not whether the shell could print it.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-14 (how a claim is run, third lesson): ask for the RIGHT place, not only a whole window; let time pass between two reports a simulator delivers at once; press the keys a person presses, not the commands behind them; after every release add a step that must move something. The replays are under `scripts/replays` on the branch `tools/decision-replays`.
+- F44: `bind` and `unbind` took only a US keyboard's key names, and compared with the shortcuts of a US keyboard. Now they go by the layout in use. Decided: a key named by what it types is written into settings.json as it was named (`ctrl+opt+ö`), not as the place it is to Arbor (`us:;`); "it ran" and "already runs" are about the key, whichever way its shortcut is written; `unbind` removes the entry that holds the key, whatever its spelling, and writes null only if a default would otherwise come back.
+- F55, `scrollOnDesktopZooms`: the window list's answer is kept for 0.3 s from when it was given, while the pointer stays within 2 points; being used does not keep it alive, because Arbor's own steps move windows under the pointer. There is no such memory for clicks (a menu may have opened meanwhile); for a notch the mistake is mild. The controller's zones are asked first, which also makes "on the Desktop" mean what it means for ⇧-drag: inside the view of a display with something docked.
